(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-dermatology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology Quiz 2 — Physical Diagnosis 2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following is a function of the skin?","choices":["Production of clotting factors","Storage of calcium for release into the blood","Filtration of nitrogenous waste from the blood","Production of vitamin D"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The skin also protects internal structures, prevents entry of microorganisms, regulates temperature and participates in excretion. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What does the term confluent mean when applied to skin lesions?","choices":["The lesions form a line or stripe","The lesions run together","The lesions remain separate from one another","The lesions form a cluster"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the opposite of discrete, where lesions remain separate. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient has intact skin over the sacrum with an area of erythema that fails to blanch with pressure. What stage of pressure ulcer is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the skin is still intact, and the non-blanching erythema is the key finding. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Generalised itching with no obvious skin lesion should prompt consideration of which causes?","choices":["Anxiety and stress, once organic causes have been excluded","Dry skin, ageing, pregnancy, uraemia, jaundice, lymphomas, leukaemias, drug reaction and lice","Dry skin and ageing only, since these account for nearly all cases","Contact dermatitis, scabies and other infestations, to the exclusion of systemic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the list spans dermatological, systemic, malignant and infestational causes. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How should psychological stress be regarded in a patient with a skin disorder?","choices":["It should be raised only after organic causes have been excluded","It is seldom the sole cause, but it can exacerbate many dermatoses","It is the single commonest cause of chronic skin disease","It has no demonstrable effect on the course of skin disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the distinction between causing and worsening is the point being made. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Lesions confined to sun-exposed skin should raise concern for which condition?","choices":["Intertrigo","Herpes zoster","Psoriasis","Skin cancers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cumulative ultraviolet exposure explains why these arise where the sun reaches. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which part of the hand should be used to assess skin temperature?","choices":["The palmar aspect","The fingertips","The ulnar border","The dorsal aspect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the back of the hand is more sensitive to temperature than the palm. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A bulla ruptures and leaves a moist area that does not bleed. How should this be described?","choices":["A fissure","An excoriation","An erosion","An ulcer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the moist, non-bleeding surface indicates the dermis has not been breached. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary skin lesion from a secondary one?","choices":["A primary lesion is larger than one centimetre in diameter, while a secondary lesion is anything smaller than that","A primary lesion is flat and a secondary lesion is elevated","A primary lesion is the one the patient noticed first","A primary lesion forms first and results directly from the disease, while a secondary lesion is a change in that lesion over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Identifying the primary lesion is the key to interpreting and describing the eruption. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is natural light preferred for inspecting the skin?","choices":["Artificial light may distort skin tone","Natural light is brighter than any artificial source available in clinic","Artificial light makes lesion borders appear larger than they are","Natural light reduces the glare produced by moist lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distortion can conceal jaundice, pallor and subtle erythema. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which descriptive terms are used for skin moisture?","choices":["Roughness and smoothness","Warmth and coolness","Mobility and turgor","Dryness, sweating and oiliness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these three cover the moisture characteristic. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A linear crack in the skin, as seen in athlete's foot, is called what?","choices":["An excoriation","A scale","A fissure","An erosion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the interdigital spaces of the foot are the classic site. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions involving the skin creases and folds?","choices":["Palmar-plantar","Intertriginous","Extensor","Photodistributed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the axillae, groin and inframammary folds are the classic intertriginous sites. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which finding defines a stage IV pressure ulcer?","choices":["Non-blanching erythema over intact skin","Full thickness skin loss with destruction of tissue, muscle and bone","Full thickness skin loss reaching but not penetrating muscle","Partial thickness loss of the epidermis and dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — involvement of muscle and bone is what separates stage IV from stage III. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Dry scaling or macerated fissuring of the interdigital spaces of the feet describes which infection?","choices":["Tinea unguium","Tinea corporis","Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the web spaces are the characteristic site. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A patient has bluish discoloration of the fingers and toes with a normal oxygen level. What does peripheral cyanosis usually reflect?","choices":["Inadequate circulation","Inadequate oxygenation in the lungs","Excess melanin production in the extremities","Chronic exposure to cold water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — peripheral cyanosis is a perfusion problem, which is why it spares the central mucous membranes. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is an atrophic scar described?","choices":["Raised and extending beyond the wound","Rough with accentuated skin lines","Thin and white","Thick and pink"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — as opposed to a hypertrophic scar, which is thick and pink. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Firm stroking of a patient's skin produces a red line, a broadening flare, and then a linear wheal. What is this called?","choices":["Nikolsky's sign","The Auspitz sign","The triple response of Lewis","The diascopy response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — capillary dilatation, arteriolar dilatation and then transudation of fluid, in that sequence. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A reddish-purple, non-blanching macule measuring two millimetres is called what?","choices":["A petechia","Purpura","An ecchymosis","A cherry angioma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — petechiae are under three millimetres. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"An elevated, irregular-shaped area of cutaneous edema that is solid, transient and of variable diameter is called what?","choices":["A plaque","A bulla","A nodule","A wheal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an allergic reaction is the example given, and transience is the distinguishing feature. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A palpable, elevated, plateau-like solid mass larger than one centimetre that occupies a large area compared with its elevation is called what?","choices":["A tumor","A patch","A plaque","A nodule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is the example, and a plaque may be coalesced papules. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What distinguishes a splinter hemorrhage from a subungual hematoma?","choices":["A splinter hemorrhage affects all nails while a subungual hematoma affects one","A splinter hemorrhage is a transverse line while a subungual hematoma is longitudinal","A splinter hemorrhage arises from the distal capillary loop, while a subungual hematoma is hemorrhage into the nail plate","A splinter hemorrhage is painful to the touch, whereas a subungual hematoma causes the patient no discomfort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the source of the bleeding is what separates the two findings. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Hair loss caused by an urge to pull out one's own hair is called what?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Androgenic alopecia","Hirsutism","Trichotillomania"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patches may be single or multiple and range from a few square centimetres to the entire scalp. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A red scaling, crusting nodule on sun-exposed skin that can ulcerate and bleed is characteristic of which malignancy?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Kaposi's sarcoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the face and other sun-exposed areas are the common sites. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which term describes grouped papules or vesicles arranged as they are in a herpes simplex infection?","choices":["Confluent","Herpetiform","Zosteriform","Annular"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the configuration is named for the infection it resembles. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the term hirsutism describe?","choices":["Excessive fine hair covering the whole body in infancy","Increased hair growth in women following a male pattern of distribution","Diffuse thinning of the scalp hair in women following the menopause","Loss of hair in multiple round patches"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pattern of distribution, not simply the amount, defines the term. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which set of questions forms the core of a proper dermatological history?","choices":["Where the problem first appeared, what it looked like, how it has progressed, associated symptoms, and what treatment has been tried","The patient's age, occupation, marital status and insurance carrier, recorded in full before the skin complaint is discussed","Whether the lesion is a macule, papule, plaque or nodule","The results of any previous biopsy, culture and blood work"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those five questions cover onset, original appearance, evolution, symptoms and prior therapy. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which gland surrounds the hair follicle and keeps hair and skin moist?","choices":["The sebaceous gland","The eccrine gland","The apocrine gland","The sudoriferous gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it secretes sebum directly into the follicle. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How should the scalp be examined?","choices":["Ask the patient to wet the hair before inspection","Separate the hair into sections in order to observe the scalp beneath","Inspect the hair from a distance without touching the patient","Palpate the scalp through the hair without visual inspection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the scalp cannot be assessed through intact hair. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A patient has central cyanosis. What is the usual explanation?","choices":["Inadequate oxygenation in the lungs","Inadequate circulation to the extremities","Increased pigmentation of the skin","Deposition of bilirubin in the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — central cyanosis points to a pulmonary problem rather than a circulatory one. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 18"}]};
